# Line endings, pinned for every checkout.
#
# The Kabmo is developed on macOS, gated on Linux, and SHIPS a Windows CLI, so
# the same working tree is checked out on all three. Without this file git uses
# each platform's default, and Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true` — which
# rewrites every text file to CRLF in the working tree. That is not cosmetic
# here: a large part of the suite compares file CONTENT byte for byte (git
# operations, the editor, the diff panel, markdown), and a CRLF checkout makes
# every one of those assertions fail for a reason that has nothing to do with
# the code under test. Measured on the first Windows run: the git merge, stash,
# discard and status suites all reported `- Expected - 0 / + Received + 0` — a
# diff whose only difference is the invisible `\r`.
#
# `text=auto` normalises to LF in the repository and lets git decide per file,
# so a genuinely binary asset is still stored as-is. `*.sh` and the CLI's own
# scripts are pinned to LF because a CRLF shebang line makes a shell script
# unrunnable on POSIX (`#!/bin/sh\r` names an interpreter that does not exist).
#
# `eol=lf` is what makes that promise hold in the WORKING TREE, and it is not
# implied by `text=auto`. `text=auto` alone normalises only the INDEX; on
# checkout git honours `core.autocrlf`, which a Windows runner sets to `true`,
# so every file arrived CRLF there while the repository said LF. Measured on the
# Windows gate: `release/linux.test.js` read `Cargo.toml` and matched
# `/\[\[bin\]\]\nname = "Kabmo"/` against `[[bin]]\r\nname = …` and failed — the
# same class of invisible-`\r` failure this file was written to prevent, still
# present because the earlier rule stopped one step short.
#
# `*.bat` is the one exception and it is deliberate: `cmd.exe` parses batch
# files line by line and has long-standing trouble with a bare LF around labels
# and `goto`, so the platform's own convention is kept for the files that run
# under it.
#
# Do not add a per-file `-text` override for anything the suite reads as text.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts and anything else executed through a shebang: LF, always.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
src/cli/** text eol=lf

# Images and other binary assets: never touch them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite-wal binary
*.sqlite-shm binary
